# Feature maps

Dmytro: "we need feature-maps in codebase in addition to tasks". A task says what to do next; a
feature map says what the product is: a tree of features, each with a status, linked to the specs,
definitions and tasks that make it up, so a reader sees what exists and what is missing
([D118](../../docs/DECISIONS.md)).

A feature map is an MDL document in the codebase, like a spec. Each `feature` block is one feature:

    feature F-2: Feature maps
      status: building
      parent: F-1
      specs: Features.Spec
      defs: Features.problems | Features.render
      tasks: 8a24ab99

`status` is `planned`, `building`, `shipped` or `dropped`; `parent` names another feature of the same
document; `specs`, `defs` and `tasks` list spec documents, definitions and task ids, separated by
`|` or one per line. Blocks are stored as `<Document>.<id>` (D99), so features branch, merge, replicate
and export with the code. `deus mdl` checks every feature block, and `deus features` prints the tree.

Written in the order of the process ([D92](../../docs/DECISIONS.md)): requirements, then the APIs and
laws, then the implementation.

## Extensions

| Term | URI |
| --- | --- |
| `api` | `org.dxos.deus.api@1.0` |
| `req` | `org.dxos.deus.req@1.0` |

## APIs

```mdl
api A-1: reading and checking features
  signatures:
  - Features.fromBlock : Block -> Result (List Text) Feature
  - Features.problems : Feature -> (Text -> {e} Result Text (List (Text, Text))) -> {e} List Text
  - Features.check : Block -> {MdlHost} List Text
```

```mdl
api A-2: the tree
  signatures:
  - Features.tree : List Feature -> List (Int, Feature)
  - Features.orphans : List Feature -> List Text
  - Features.render : List Feature -> (Text -> {e} Result Text (List (Text, Text))) -> {e} Text
```

## What a feature block must say

A status outside the four is a mistake, not a fifth status. Every link must resolve: a definition by
name, a spec document by name, a task by id.

```mdl
req R-1: a feature's status is planned, building, shipped or dropped
  by: Features.Spec.statusIsChecked | Features.Spec.statusIsRequired
```

```mdl
req R-2: every linked definition, spec document and task exists
  by: Features.Spec.missingLinksReported
```

## What shipped means

A shipped feature is finished: nothing it links is still to do. An open task or a definition declared
without a body (D94) contradicts the status, so `deus mdl` fails until one of them changes.

```mdl
req R-3: a shipped feature has no open task
  by: Features.Spec.shippedNeedsClosedTasks
```

```mdl
req R-4: a shipped feature has no unimplemented definition
  by: Features.Spec.shippedNeedsImplementedDefs
```

## What is missing

A feature map is most useful for what it shows is missing. Two things are reported as known gaps,
which do not fail the document: a feature linked to nothing, and a planned or building feature with no
open task, since nobody is on the hook for the rest of it.

```mdl
req R-5: a feature linked to nothing is a known gap
  by: Features.Spec.unlinkedIsGap
```

```mdl
req R-6: unfinished work with no open task is a known gap
  by: Features.Spec.unfinishedWithoutTaskIsGap | Features.Spec.unfinishedWithTaskIsFine
```

## The tree

`deus features` prints each document's features depth first, children under their parent in the
order the document gives them, each with its status and how many of its tasks are open. A parent
the document does not have is reported, and its children print as roots so nothing is hidden.

```mdl
req R-7: features print depth first under their parents
  by: Features.Spec.treeNestsChildren | Features.Spec.renderShowsStatusAndOpenTasks
```

```mdl
req R-8: a feature whose parent is missing is reported and still printed
  by: Features.Spec.orphansReported | Features.Spec.orphansPrintAsRoots
```

## Laws

The laws run against a made-up codebase: `facts` answers `MdlHost.describe` the way the CLI does for
two definitions, two tasks and one spec document.
